hypotheses.statement:
Conformal invariance of the Dirichlet integral and the Dirichlet inequality on $\Omega$ give $$\label{eq:subcritical}
\lVert \psi\rVert_{L^2(q)}^2\le d\int_\mathbb D|\nabla\psi|^2,
\qquad \psi\in H^1_0(\mathbb D),\qquad d=\frac\mu{\lambda_D}<1.$$

For $p\in\mathbb D$ and distinct $s,t\in\mathbb S^1$, define $$\begin{aligned}
K(p,s)&=P_s(p)+\sum_{n\ge1}(T^nP_s)(p),\label{eq:K}\\
R(s,t)&=\sum_{n\ge0}\int_\mathbb DP_s\,T^nP_t\,\mathrm dq,\label{eq:R}\\
N(s,t)&=N_0(s,t)+R(s,t),\qquad N_0(s,t)=\frac1{\pi|s-t|^2}.\label{eq:N}
\end{aligned}$$

For $0\le\rho\le q$ supported on a compact subset of $\mathbb D$, let $T_\rho$ be the Green operator on $L^2(\rho)$ and define $$\label{eq:M-rho}
M^\rho(x,y)=G(x,y)
+\langle G(x,\cdot),(I-T_\rho)^{-1}G(y,\cdot)\rangle_\rho.$$

outputs.exact_statement:
For tangent $X\in H^1(\Omega;\mathbb R^2)$, $$\label{eq:curvature}
\mathcal Q(X)=\int_\Omega|\nabla X|^2
+\int_{\partial\Omega}\kappa|X|^2.$$

The kernels above are finite at the stated points. For fixed $p$, $K(p,\cdot)$ is positive and bounded. The kernel $R$ is symmetric, nonnegative, and integrable on $\mathbb S^1\times\mathbb S^1$, with $$\label{eq:R-integral}
\iint R(s,t)\,\mathrm ds\,\mathrm dt\le\frac{q(\mathbb D)}{1-d}.$$ For Lipschitz boundary data, $$\begin{aligned}
W_h(p)&=\int_{\mathbb S^1}K(p,s)h(s)\,\mathrm ds,\label{eq:K-representation}\\
\langle Ah,(I-T)^{-1}Ak\rangle_q
&=\iint R(s,t)h(s)\cdot k(t)\,\mathrm ds\,\mathrm dt.\label{eq:R-representation}
\end{aligned}$$

For every real Lipschitz function $b$ on $\mathbb S^1$, $$\label{eq:multiplier}
E(bg)=\frac12\iint N(s,t)(b(s)-b(t))^2
g(s)\cdot g(t)\,\mathrm ds\,\mathrm dt.$$

For distinct $s_1,\ldots,s_m\in\mathbb S^1$ and $p\in\mathbb D$, the matrix $$\label{eq:bordered}
\begin{pmatrix}B&\ell\\\ell^T&0\end{pmatrix},
\qquad
B_{ij}=\begin{cases}1/N(s_i,s_j),&i\ne j,\\0,&i=j,\end{cases}
\qquad \ell_i=1/K(p,s_i),$$ has <span class="upright">(P)</span>.

Let $n\ge2$ be an integer, let $p,q>0$, and let $A,B\in\mathbb R$. Suppose that $$\label{eq:subcritical}
 \frac{n+A}{p+1}+\frac{n+B}{q+1}>n-2.$$ There are no functions $$u,v\in C^2(\mathbb R^n\setminus\{0\})\cap C(\mathbb R^n),
 \qquad u(x)>0,\quad v(x)>0\quad(x\in\mathbb R^n),$$ satisfying Equation <a href="#eq:system" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:system">[eq:system]</a> at every $x\ne0$.

For every solution with $n\ge3$ and $A,B>-2$, the sources $f,g$ belong to $L^1_{\mathrm{loc}}(\mathbb R^n)$ and $$-\Delta u=g,\qquad -\Delta v=f
 \quad\hbox{in distributions on }\mathbb R^n.$$ The Newton potentials are finite everywhere and satisfy $$\label{eq:potential-lower}
 K*g\le u,\qquad K*f\le v \quad\hbox{on }\mathbb R^n.$$ There are constants $c_u,c_v\ge0$ such that $$\label{eq:potential-constants}
 u=c_u+K*g,\qquad v=c_v+K*f$$ almost everywhere on $\mathbb R^n$ and pointwise on $\mathbb R^n\setminus\{0\}$. On the latter set the potentials are continuously differentiable, and $$\label{eq:potential-gradients}
 \begin{split}
 \nabla u(x)&=\int_{\mathbb R^n}\nabla K(x-y)g(y)\,\,\mathrm dy,\\
 \nabla v(x)&=\int_{\mathbb R^n}\nabla K(x-y)f(y)\,\,\mathrm dy,\\
 \nabla K(z)&=-(n-2)\frac{z}{|z|^2}K(z).
 \end{split}$$ Both gradient integrals converge absolutely at every $x\ne0$.

Fix $n\ge3$, $p,q>0$, $A,B>-2$, and $pq>1$. There is a universal constant $C$ such that every solution satisfies, for all $R>0$, $$\label{eq:source-bounds}
 \int_{B_R}f\le CR^{n-2-\beta},\qquad
 \int_{B_R}g\le CR^{n-2-\alpha}.$$ The constants in <a href="#eq:potential-constants" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:potential-constants">[eq:potential-constants]</a> vanish, so that $$\label{eq:potentials}
 u=K*g,\qquad v=K*f\quad\hbox{on }\mathbb R^n\setminus\{0\}.$$ Moreover, $$\label{eq:uniform}
 \begin{split}
 &\int_{B_4}(f+g+u+v)\le C,\\
 &\int_{|x-y|\ge\ell}K(x-y)(f(y)+g(y))\,\,\mathrm dy
       \le C\ell^{2-n}
       \qquad (x\in B_2,\ 0<\ell\le1).
 \end{split}$$

For the cap weights <a href="#eq:cap-weight" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:cap-weight">[eq:cap-weight]</a> and component weights <a href="#eq:component-weight" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:component-weight">[eq:component-weight]</a>, under the hypotheses of Proposition <a href="#prop:pressure" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="prop:pressure">10</a>, every $P\ge1$ satisfies $$\label{eq:layer-loss}
 \mathbb E_e\bigl(L_f(e)+L_g(e)\bigr)
 \le\bigl(C_\kappa\delta+C_{\kappa,\delta}P^{-1}\bigr)E+CP.$$ The constants in the relative coefficient are independent of $P$, and the last constant is independent of $\kappa,\delta,P$.

For the fixed equation parameters there is a constant $C$, independent of the solution pair, such that $$\label{eq:energy-bound}
 \int_{B_1}\bigl(|x|^B u^{q+1}+|x|^A v^{p+1}\bigr)\,\mathrm dx
 \le E\le C.$$
